Towns A and B are $180$ km apart.
Bea leaves town A at $9$ am and drives to town B at a steady speed, arriving at noon.
At the same time Cal leaves town B for town A: he drives at $120$ km/h for $1$ hour, stops for $30$ minutes, then drives on at $120$ km/h and reaches town A at $11$ am.
Draw both journeys on the grid, measuring distance from town A.

mastery.solution_text:
Bea: $(9{:}00,\,0)$ to $(12{:}00,\,180\text{ km})$
Cal's first hour: $120\times1=120$ km, so at $10$ am he is $180-120=60$ km from town A.
The stop holds him at $60$ km until $10{:}30$.
Last leg: $\frac{60}{120}=0.5$ h, reaching town A at $11$ am.
Cal: $(9{:}00,\,180)$, $(10{:}00,\,60)$, $(10{:}30,\,60)$, $(11{:}00,\,0)$
